<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jeju Island</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jejudo, Quelpart</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jeju Island is a volcanic island off the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, the country's largest island, dominated by Mount Hallasan. It was the seat of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timna-yemen">Tamna</a> before its absorption by Goryeo, and in 1273 the last Sambyeolcho rebels were crushed there. In the Jeju uprising and its suppression in 1948–1949 tens of thousands of islanders were killed. It is now a self-governing province and a major resort.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeju-island.jpg" alt="Jeju Island" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Simmon, using Landsat data provided by the United States Geological Survey. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jeju_Island.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jeju Island lies in the Korea Strait, 82.8 km south of the nearest point on the Korean Peninsula. It covers 1,833.2 km2, which is 1.83% of the area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, and has an oval shape about 73 km from east to west, with a gentle slope around Hallasan in the centre. The coastline is 258 km long. The island consists chiefly of basalt and lava, and Hallasan, a volcano 1,947 metres high, is the highest mountain in South Korea.</p><p>The island formed from volcanic eruptions about two million years ago, in the Pleistocene epoch. A magma chamber formed under the sea floor about 1.2 million years ago and began to erupt, and activity stopped for about 100 thousand years before it restarted along the coast about 300 thousand years ago. Hallasan took shape about 100 thousand years ago, and lateral eruptions about 25 thousand years ago left some 360 oreum, small extinct parasitic cones such as Geomunoreum and Yongnuni Oreum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Jeju people are indigenous to the island, which has been populated by modern humans since the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> period. Its many historical names include Juho, Tammora, Seomna, Tangna and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timna-yemen">Tamna</a>, and later Samdado, the Island of Three Abundances. Before the Japanese annexation of 1910 Europeans usually called it Quelpart, a name from French attested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> by 1648, which may come from a Dutch ship, the quelpaert de Brack, said to have first sighted the island around 1642. The first European explorers to sight it, the Portuguese, called it Ilha de Ladrones, the Island of Thieves. Under Japanese occupation it was known as Saishū, and Cheju remained the usual spelling until 7 July 2000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Jeju forms the majority of Jeju Province together with its outlying islands. It has a humid subtropical climate with four seasons, cool winters and hot, very wet summers, and the south around Seogwipo is warmer in winter than Jeju City because Hallasan blocks the continental winds. About 12% of the island is gotjawal, forest on rough lava that stayed uncultivated until the 21st century and is the main source of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a> for the island's half million people.</p><p>The Jeju language is considered critically endangered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>, and the island is one of the regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> where shamanism is most intact. It contains the natural World Heritage Site named Jeju Volcanic Island and Lava Tubes, and tourism is a large part of its economy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jeju Island” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The court returned to Kaegyong in 1270, provoking the revolt of the Sambyeolcho units, crushed on <b>Jeju</b> in 1273.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…Mumun culture that Korean archaeologists call Songguk-ri Culture is found in the Hoseo and Honam regions, western Yeongnam, the Ulsan and Gimhae areas and, since a find in 2005, deep in the interior of Gangwon Province; its farthest reach appears to have been <b>Jeju Island</b> and western Japan.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mumun-culture">Mumun culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Survivors under Kim Tong-jeong held out on <b>Jeju</b> until 1273.</blockquote><a href="/state/sambyeolcho">Sambyeolcho</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jeju</b> legend tells of three founders, ancestors of the Go, Yang and Bu clans, who emerged from holes in the ground at Samseonghyeol and married princesses who arrived by sea.</blockquote><a href="/state/tamna">Tamna</a></li><li><blockquote>The United States took the Korean question to the United Nations, which supervised elections in the south in May 1948, amid the <b>Jeju</b> uprising.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-states-army-military-government-in-korea">United States Army Military Government in Korea</a></li></ul></section>
